      "writtenOn": "2024-01-30T19:25:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With this patch, this is how we slow down the scheduling. I made an attempt prior to do the scheduling in the motion sense loop. The issue I had there was that it was hard to tell how long to sleep for. As an example, if the `ec_rate` wasn\u0027t 0, sleeping for `ec_rate` as the duration was incorrect, because it didn\u0027t take into account other sensors which \n\nWhat I tried was modifying this line:\n```c\ntime_diff \u003d time_until(ts_end_task.le.lo, sensor-\u003enext_collection);\n```\n\nTo look more like:\n```c\ntime_diff \u003d MAX(time_until(ts_end_task.le.lo, sensor-\u003enext_collection),\n                sensor-\u003econfig[motion_sense_get_ec_config()].ec_rate);\n```\n\nThe issue I saw was that this takes the delta from when the task finished. So we were actually sleeping too long. Alternatively, I could check the time with `ec_rate` from `ts_start_task`. But there was an issue there too, I had to be sure that the reason we started the task was the previous `ec_rate` timer. It\u0027s possible that we called to sleep for `ec_rate` duration but woke up early and did\u0027t collect a sample since we weren\u0027t sleeping enough. Then we would schedule the sleep again, but for too long.\n\nUsing the existing infrastructure of `next_collection` made these edge cases simpler to navigate since we already handled them.",
      "parentUuid": "53fafb3d_5b76acd7",
      "revId": "f31ae1e52dad2ca0c4159f716c5c680b15c36892",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
